editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:87:"Ask how much paper." Brom took the pencil out of his hand and turned the notebook round. "A stall that size takes a quire in the morning and sends the boy for another at noon. I've watched him. A quire's twenty-four sheets." He wrote it down in his large slow figures. "If he's three times what he was, he's on three quires. The other two towns are bigger. Say four each, to be safe." He did not ask how Cael knew the towns; he only worked down the page, a line at a time, the way he loaded a cart, with the heaviest things at the bottom and each line lashed to the one beneath it before he trusted it with the next. "There. Two weeklies. That's your copper."
